> phi 1
phi(1) = 1
  how: 1 = 1; phi = product of p^(e-1) (p - 1): 1 (the empty product)
  the order of the unit group (Z/1)*; lambda(1) = 1 is its exponent
  check: counted the k in 1..1 with gcd(k, 1) = 1: 1
> phi 12
phi(12) = 4
  how: 12 = 2^2 * 3; phi = product of p^(e-1) (p - 1): 2^1*1 * 2
  the order of the unit group (Z/12)*; lambda(12) = 2 is its exponent
  check: counted the k in 1..12 with gcd(k, 12) = 1: 4
> phi 60
phi(60) = 16
  how: 60 = 2^2 * 3 * 5; phi = product of p^(e-1) (p - 1): 2^1*1 * 2 * 4
  the order of the unit group (Z/60)*; lambda(60) = 4 is its exponent
  check: counted the k in 1..60 with gcd(k, 60) = 1: 16
> phi 100
phi(100) = 40
  how: 100 = 2^2 * 5^2; phi = product of p^(e-1) (p - 1): 2^1*1 * 5^1*4
  the order of the unit group (Z/100)*; lambda(100) = 20 is its exponent
  check: counted the k in 1..100 with gcd(k, 100) = 1: 40
> phi 561
phi(561) = 320
  how: 561 = 3 * 11 * 17; phi = product of p^(e-1) (p - 1): 2 * 10 * 16
  the order of the unit group (Z/561)*; lambda(561) = 80 is its exponent
  check: counted the k in 1..561 with gcd(k, 561) = 1: 320
> phi 2147483647
phi(2147483647) = 2147483646
  how: 2147483647 = 2147483647; phi = product of p^(e-1) (p - 1): 2147483646
  the order of the unit group (Z/2147483647)*; lambda(2147483647) = 2147483646 is its exponent
> carmichael 1
lambda(1) = 1 (Carmichael: the least m with a^m = 1 for every unit a)
  how: 1 = 1; lambda is the lcm of nothing: 1
  lambda(2^e) = 2^(e-2) for e >= 3; lambda(p^e) = phi(p^e) otherwise; phi(1) = 1
  check: the lcm of the orders of all units, by stepping through their powers: 1
> carmichael 4
lambda(4) = 2 (Carmichael: the least m with a^m = 1 for every unit a)
  how: 4 = 2^2; lambda is the lcm of 2 (2^2)
  lambda(2^e) = 2^(e-2) for e >= 3; lambda(p^e) = phi(p^e) otherwise; phi(4) = 2
  check: the lcm of the orders of all units, by stepping through their powers: 2
> carmichael 12
lambda(12) = 2 (Carmichael: the least m with a^m = 1 for every unit a)
  how: 12 = 2^2 * 3; lambda is the lcm of 2 (2^2), 2 (3^1)
  lambda(2^e) = 2^(e-2) for e >= 3; lambda(p^e) = phi(p^e) otherwise; phi(12) = 4
  check: the lcm of the orders of all units, by stepping through their powers: 2
> carmichael 561
lambda(561) = 80 (Carmichael: the least m with a^m = 1 for every unit a)
  how: 561 = 3 * 11 * 17; lambda is the lcm of 2 (3^1), 10 (11^1), 16 (17^1)
  lambda(2^e) = 2^(e-2) for e >= 3; lambda(p^e) = phi(p^e) otherwise; phi(561) = 320
  check: the lcm of the orders of all units, by stepping through their powers: 80
> carmichael 1000000007
lambda(1000000007) = 1000000006 (Carmichael: the least m with a^m = 1 for every unit a)
  how: 1000000007 = 1000000007; lambda is the lcm of 1000000006 (1000000007^1)
  lambda(2^e) = 2^(e-2) for e >= 3; lambda(p^e) = phi(p^e) otherwise; phi(1000000007) = 1000000006
